WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.875
Welcome back.

2
00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:06.400
Today we start the steering rules proper: who keeps out of whose way.

3
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:12.175
But before any of them, Rule eleven sets a condition, and it’s one sentence long.

4
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:17.550
Rules eleven through eighteen apply only to vessels in sight of one another.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.400
Remember that phrase from lesson two?

6
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.025
In sight means seen with the eyes.

7
00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:28.825
So if you’re in fog and the other vessel isn’t in sight, none of today’s rules apply.

8
00:00:29.075 --> 00:00:32.575
There’s no stand-on vessel and no give-way vessel.

9
00:00:32.825 --> 00:00:37.175
Rule nineteen takes over instead, and we’ll get to it in lesson eight.

10
00:00:37.425 --> 00:00:41.875
And a radar contact alone never makes two vessels in sight.

11
00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:43.925
Here’s a quick test.

12
00:00:44.175 --> 00:00:49.400
You’re running in thick fog, and your radar shows a boat crossing ahead from your right.

13
00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.800
Are you the give-way vessel?

14
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.175
No.

15
00:00:54.425 --> 00:00:56.200
Nobody is.

16
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.000
You can’t see her, so the crossing rule doesn’t apply.

17
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:05.300
Hold on to that idea, because the exam asks it a dozen different ways.
